## 2026-07-27 - US-014

Resolve Node builtins and Electron externals in the Vite build.

**Interpretation:** a build-configuration story, so per execution rule 3
no failing test was written first -- there is no unit test runner until
US-019 and nothing behavioural to assert. The gate is the build's own
diagnostics plus targeted empirical probes, which is what the
acceptance criteria ask for. Two criteria ("confirmed by grep") are
findings to record, not code to write.

**What was implemented**

`vite.config.js`:

- `path` aliased to `path-browserify` 1.0.1 (new devDependency, exact
  pin), in both the `path` and `node:path` spellings. Bundled, not
  externalized, per FR-7, so its 13 first-party call sites need no
  change and no Phase 2 shim.
- `build.rollupOptions.output.format = 'cjs'`. This is the load-bearing
  change: without it the externals below cannot be emitted as
  `require()` and would not resolve under Electron.
- `rollupOptions.external` built from two deliberately separate lists.
  `firstPartyExternals`: `fs`, `http`, `events`, `electron`,
  `@electron/remote`. `transitiveExternals` (dependency-only, no
  renderer importer): `assert`, `constants`, `dgram`, `fs/promises`,
  `https`, `net`, `os`, `querystring`, `stream`, `timers`, `tls`,
  `url`, `util`, `worker_threads`. Both spellings of each builtin.
- `require.resolve` via `createRequire(import.meta.url)` so depcheck
  can see the `path-browserify` usage.

`events` is not named in the acceptance criteria but is imported by
`src/renderer/lib/state.js`, and a stub there throws on the first state
event, so it got the same treatment as `fs`. The transitive list is
likewise beyond the letter of the criteria: without it the config-save
and cast-discovery paths would be wired to throwing stubs the moment
US-015/US-016 make the bundle the one that actually loads. The two
lists are kept separate so the first-party one stays a faithful
inventory of Phase 2 shim scope.

`docs/vite-baseline.md`: new section documenting the resolution
strategy per builtin, including the two grep findings the criteria ask
to record, and the Electron probe output.

**Files changed**

- `vite.config.js`
- `package.json`, `package-lock.json` (path-browserify devDependency)
- `docs/vite-baseline.md`
- `src/AGENTS.md` (new "Node builtins in `src/renderer/`" section)
- `progress.log`, `specs/prd.json`

**Verification**

- `npx vite build`: exit 0, 713 modules, **0 unresolved imports** and
  **0 `has been externalized for browser compatibility` warnings**
  (was 113). Remaining stderr is a direct-`eval` warning from
  `@protobufjs/inquire` and the >500 kB chunk-size notice, both
  pre-existing and neither a resolution failure.
- Output bundle inspected, not just its exit code: no `import`/`export`
  statements, 19 distinct `require("...")` specifiers covering every
  external, **no `require("path")`**, and the `path-browserify`-unique
  literal `Path must be a string` present -- so `path` is bundled and
  everything else is externalized, as intended.
- `crypto`: grep over `src/` returns one hit,
  `src/renderer/webtorrent.js:5`, which FR-4 exempts from the Vite
  build. No external entry, confirmed by its absence from the warning
  stream. `os`: grep returns zero hits in `src/`; the only renderer
  importer was the telemetry module US-002 deleted.
- Electron resolution verified empirically, since section 5 of the
  baseline is precisely the finding that a green build proves nothing
  here. A temporary `web-contents-created` hook in
  `build/main/index.js` evaluated `require('electron')` in both live
  renderers: `hasIpcRenderer: "object"`, `hasRemote: "object"`,
  `fsExists: "function"`. Probe patched build artifacts only and was
  removed by the following `npm run build`.
- `npm test` (standard + depcheck): exit 0.
- `npm run build` (Babel, FR-2): 64 files, still green.
- Launch check: `init: 743ms`, both windows, no `getCurrentWindow`
  error this run.
- **The six-step manual smoke test was NOT run** -- non-interactive
  session. It also would not exercise this change yet: the app still
  loads the Babel build from `static/main.html`, and nothing points at
  the Vite bundle until US-015/US-016. The risk this story carries is
  therefore deferred to US-017, and it is concentrated in the
  externals that no probe here exercised -- the config-persistence
  stack under `lib/state.js` and the casting stack under `lib/cast.js`.

**Learnings for future iterations:**

- **`output.format: 'cjs'` is what makes an external real.** Marking a
  module external only records "do not bundle this"; something has to
  emit a working `require()` at the other end. Under the default ESM
  output the same config produces `import` statements a classic
  `<script>` renderer cannot execute. Verify by grepping the bundle,
  not by reading the config.
- **Count the externalization warnings, every time.** Going from 113 to
  0 was the whole story, and the exit code was 0 both before and after.
- **A separate list beats a `builtinModules` catch-all.** Externalizing
  every builtin wholesale would work and would also erase the
  distinction between "the renderer needs this" and "a dependency needs
  this" -- which is exactly the inventory Phase 2 is scoped from. It
  would also absorb a new dependency's builtin silently.
- **Acceptance criteria can under-specify a build fix.** `events` and
  the 14 dependency-only builtins were not listed but would have been
  live throwing stubs the moment the bundle is actually loaded. When a
  criterion enumerates examples of a class, measure the whole class.
- **Probe Electron resolution in the renderer, never in Node.** In a
  plain Node process `require('electron')` returns the CLI helper's
  path string; inside a renderer with `nodeIntegration: true` the same
  call returns the runtime API. Only the second one answers the
  question, and `wc.executeJavaScript` from a `web-contents-created`
  hook is how to ask it non-interactively.
