## 2026-07-27 - US-015

Serve the renderer HTML through the Vite bundle.

**Interpretation:** no failing test was written first. This repo still
has no unit test runner (it arrives in US-019) and the change is one
markup line plus build config; the meaningful verification is
empirical, so the evidence below is a launch check plus a DOM/CSS probe
of the running renderer.

**What was implemented**

- `static/main.html` now loads the Vite output with a plain
  `<script src="../build-vite/renderer/main.js">` instead of the inline
  `require('../build/renderer/main.js')`. The tag stays in the same
  position, at the end of `<body>` after `<div id='body'>`, so
  `document.getElementById('body')` is present when the bundle runs.
- `vite.config.js` pins the entry filename to `renderer/main.js`
  (`output.entryFileNames`). The default `assets/main-[hash].js`
  changes on every build and cannot be referenced from a static
  `<script src>`. Chunks and assets keep their hashes -- nothing links
  to them by name.
- `vite.config.js` adds `define: { 'process.env': 'globalThis.process.env' }`.
  This was the one real bug the story surfaced: see below.
- `static/webtorrent.html` and `static/main.css` are untouched
  (`git diff --stat` lists only `static/main.html` and
  `vite.config.js`).

**Files changed**

- `static/main.html`, `vite.config.js`, `src/AGENTS.md`,
  `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`

**Verification**

- `npx vite build`: 0 `has been externalized` warnings, 0 lines
  matching `^import|^export` in the output, and the emitted `require()`
  set is exactly the configured external list.
- Launch check with `npx electron --no-sandbox .`: the log shows
  `'Main Window'` and the main window's `init:` line. The only
  severity-3 renderer error is the pre-existing
  `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'getCurrentWindow')`
  from `electron.remote`, which is on master.
- DOM/CSS probe via `wc.executeJavaScript` after load:
  `{"url":".../static/main.html","hasHeader":true,"bodyLen":29047,
  "css":"rgb(40, 40, 40)"}`. `.header` present and a 29 kB body means
  the React tree rendered from the Vite bundle;
  `getComputedStyle(document.body).backgroundColor` matching
  `main.css:13` (`background: rgb(40, 40, 40)`) is the evidence that
  `main.css` is still applied. `main.html` has no inline background, so
  the value can only come from the stylesheet.
- `npm test` (standard + depcheck) passes.
- **Smoke test not run.** This session is non-interactive. The launch
  check and the DOM probe cover "the window opens and renders from the
  Vite bundle"; what they do not cover is interaction -- adding a
  torrent, playback, the menus. Since this is the first commit where
  anything actually executes the Vite output, that is where the
  residual risk sits, and US-017 is the verification pass that owns it.

**Learnings for future iterations**

- **Vite rewrites bare `process.env` to `{}`.** It is a browser-first
  default and it is silent: `application-config-path` compiled to
  `path.join({}["HOME"], ...)`, so the very first `path.join` of
  startup threw `Path must be a string. Received undefined` and the
  whole renderer died before rendering anything. The fix is
  `define: { 'process.env': 'globalThis.process.env' }` -- going
  through `globalThis` hands back the real object without re-triggering
  the substitution. `process.env.NODE_ENV` is a longer key and still
  wins, so mode-dependent library code is unaffected. Expect the same
  class of silent substitution for any other Node-ism Vite assumes is
  absent in a browser.
- **Getting a stack trace out of the renderer.** The `console-message`
  hook gives a message and no stack. To get the stack, temporarily add
  an inline `<script>window.addEventListener('error', e =>
  console.log('STACK:' + e.error.stack))</script>` to `static/main.html`
  *before* the bundle tag, and build with
  `npx vite build --minify false` so the frames name real functions.
  Terminal output mangles the escaped `\n`; pipe through
  `tr '\\n' '\n'` to read it.
- **Probing the DOM needs a delay after `did-finish-load`.** The event
  fires before the React tree exists; a `setTimeout(..., 4000)` inside
  the handler is what makes the probe meaningful. Both windows fire it,
  so key the assertion on `location.pathname` -- the hidden
  `webtorrent.html` legitimately reports a 53-char body and no
  `.header`.
- **Background colour is a usable CSS-applied assertion** here because
  `main.html` sets no inline background: a computed
  `rgb(40, 40, 40)` can only have come from `main.css`. Check the
  stylesheet actually declares the value you assert on before treating
  a match as proof -- `#282828` appears in `webtorrent.html`'s inline
  style too, and that coincidence would have made the check vacuous.
---

## 2026-07-27 - US-016

Wire the npm scripts to the Vite build.

**Interpretation:** no failing test was written first. Per execution
rule 3 this is a pure build-configuration story, and the repo still has
no unit test runner (US-019 owns that). The meaningful verification is
empirical, so the evidence below is a from-scratch build, a launch
check, a real `electron-packager` run with an asar inventory, and a
timed `npm run watch` observation.

**What was implemented**

- `npm run build` is now
  `npm run build:main && npm run build:renderer`.
  - `build:main` is the old `babel src --out-dir build` plus an
    `--ignore` list covering the four paths Vite now owns exclusively:
    `src/renderer/main.js`, `components/**`, `controllers/**`,
    `pages/**`. Babel still compiles `src/config.js`, `src/main/**`,
    `src/renderer/webtorrent.js` and `src/renderer/lib/**` (38 files),
    which is exactly the set something still `require()`s: `index.js`
    -> `build/main`, `static/webtorrent.html` -> `build/renderer/
    webtorrent.js`, and `src/main/index.js` -> `../renderer/lib/state`.
  - `build:renderer` is `vite build`.
- `npm start` is unchanged (`npm run build && electron ...`); it now
  picks up the Vite output because `build` does.
- `npm run watch` gains `--ignore build-vite/`. Without it nodemon --
  which already watches `--ext js,css` -- would see the freshly emitted
  `build-vite/renderer/main.js` and restart itself forever. `build/`
  was already ignored for the same reason.
- `npm test` gains `build-vite` to `--ignore-dirs`, matching `build`
  and `dist`. depcheck passed either way; this just keeps it from
  parsing a 1.4 MB bundle.
- `bin/package.js` nukes `build-vite/` alongside `build/` and `dist/`
  before building, via a new `BUILD_VITE_PATH`. The packager `ignore`
  regex needed no change -- verified by running it against
  `/build-vite/renderer/main.js`.
- `bin/clean.js` removes `build-vite/` too.
- `src/AGENTS.md`: the "Module systems" section said `npm run build` is
  `babel src --out-dir build`, which is no longer true. Rewritten to
  describe both halves and why the ignore list is what it is.

**Files changed**

- `package.json`, `bin/package.js`, `bin/clean.js`, `src/AGENTS.md`,
  `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`

**Verification**

- From-scratch `rm -rf build build-vite && npm run build`: exit 0,
  Babel reports 38 files, Vite emits `build-vite/renderer/main.js`.
  `build/main/index.js`, `build/config.js` and
  `build/renderer/webtorrent.js` are present; `build/renderer/main.js`
  is correctly absent.
- Bundle invariants held: 0 `has been externalized` warnings, 0 lines
  matching `^import|^export`, and the emitted `require()` set is
  exactly the configured external list.
- `npm start` launch check: process alive after 14s, log shows
  `'Main Window'` and `init: 4.456s`.
- `npm run package`, the packager step: run for real via a throwaway
  script that reuses `bin/package.js`'s exact `all` + `darwin` option
  objects, skipping only the `rimraf(node_modules)` + `npm ci`
  prelude. `electron-packager` succeeded. `asar list` on the resulting
  `app.asar` confirms `/build-vite/renderer/main.js`,
  `/build/main/index.js`, `/build/renderer/webtorrent.js`,
  `/static/main.html` and `/index.js` are all inside. Launching the
  packaged `WebTorrent.app` binary: alive after 15s, log shows
  `'Main Window'`, `init: 4.278s`, and `'WebTorrent Hidden Window'` --
  so both the Vite half and the Babel half work from inside the asar.
- `npm run watch`: started from a clean tree, 0 restarts in the first
  30s (proving `--ignore build-vite/` works), then `touch
  src/renderer/components/header.js` produced exactly 1 restart, with
  both halves re-running (2 `Successfully compiled` from Babel, 2
  `built in` from Vite). Killed afterwards; no stray processes left.
- `npm test` (standard + depcheck) passes.
- **Smoke test not run.** Non-interactive session. The launch checks
  cover "the window opens and renders" for both the dev build and the
  packaged bundle; what they do not cover is interaction -- adding a
  torrent, playback, the menus. US-017 is the Phase 1 verification pass
  that owns that risk.

**Learnings for future iterations**

- **`npm run package` cannot be run as-is in a session:** `build()` in
  `bin/package.js` starts with `rimraf(node_modules)` + `npm ci`. To
  verify the packaging path, copy the `all` + `darwin` option objects
  into a throwaway script and call `electronPackager` directly. That
  exercises the parts a build-config change can actually break (the
  `ignore` regex, what lands in the asar) without a several-minute
  reinstall. `prune: true` operates on electron-packager's own temp
  copy, so it does not touch the working `node_modules`.
- **`asar list` is the real proof that a new build directory ships.**
  The `ignore` regex in `bin/package.js` is a single 700-char
  alternation with `^\/src|^\/dist` anchors and a trailing
  `\.[^/]*|.*\.md` group; reading it is not verification. Either test
  the regex against literal paths in `node -e`, or list the asar.
- **A new generated directory needs four registrations, not one:**
  `.gitignore`, nodemon's `--ignore` list in the `watch` script
  (anything under `--ext js,css` otherwise causes an infinite restart
  loop), depcheck's `--ignore-dirs`, and the `rimraf` block in
  `bin/package.js`. `bin/clean.js` is a fifth. Missing the nodemon one
  is the only silent-at-build-time, broken-at-dev-time failure.
- **Babel's `--ignore` takes one comma-separated string of globs** and
  a bare directory name does not match files inside it -- use
  `dir/**/*`. Verify by compiling to a temp `--out-dir` and diffing the
  file list; the exit code is 0 whether the ignore matched anything or
  not.
- **The set of files Babel must still compile is defined by the
  non-Vite `require()` callers**, and there are exactly three today:
  `index.js` -> `build/main`, `static/webtorrent.html` ->
  `build/renderer/webtorrent.js`, and `src/main/index.js` ->
  `../renderer/lib/state`. That last one is easy to miss and is why
  `src/renderer/lib/**` is built by both Vite and Babel.
